# Why is a used car worth less the moment you drive it away?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "How can hidden quality make a market unravel? What did Akerlof's \"market for lemons\" explain beyond cars?"
next: {"title":"Why can insurance markets fall apart?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/49-why-can-insurance-markets-fall-apart.cast"}

## Peach or lemon?
sources:
    - id: akerlof1970
      title: 'The Market for "Lemons": Quality Uncertainty and the Market Mechanism'
      authors: George A. Akerlof
      year: 1970
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 84(3): 488–500. Opens with the large price difference between new cars and those just off the showroom; ''the usual lunch table justification for this phenomenon is the pure joy of owning a "new" car. We offer a different explanation'': sellers know more about quality than buyers.'

    icon car size 330 set twemoji x 330 y 430 icon_look picture of automobile hidden true
    box tag_new "$30,000 new" x 740 y 500 width 280 height 86 radius 12 font_size 36 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    box tag_week "a week later: $ ?" x 740 y 370 width 320 height 86 radius 12 font_size 36 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    portrait akerlof cites ["akerlof1970"] x 850 y 470 width 190 of "George Akerlof" cameo true hidden true
    text quote "“the pure joy of owning a ‘new’ car”" #3d3833 cites ["akerlof1970"] x 500 y 205 font_size 34 style.italic true hidden true
    text quote_by "— the lunch-table answer (Akerlof, 1970)" #8f887c x 500 y 165 font_size 24 hidden true
    icon peach size 125 set twemoji x 220 y 570 icon_look picture of peach hidden true
    icon lemon size 125 set twemoji x 440 y 570 icon_look picture of lemon hidden true
    text or "or" #8f887c x 330 y 570 font_size 36 hidden true
    text owner_cue "only the owner knows" #3d3833 x 330 y 205 font_size 34 hidden true
    icon buyer size 170 set twemoji x 770 y 450 icon_look picture of person hidden true
    text buyer_cue "pays for an average car" #2f6b8f x 770 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true
    text stay_cue "peaches stay home" #b5482e x 330 y 205 font_size 34 hidden true
    mark quote_x #b5482e target ["quote"] kind strike hidden true

    card title "Peach or lemon?"

Why is a car worth less the moment you drive it away? Say (@draw tag_new@) it costs thirty thousand dollars new.
    draw car

B: Drive it for a week, put it up for sale, (@draw tag_week@) and the offers come in thousands lower.

B: Easy. A week of wear and tear.
    highlight car effect circle

    quiz "Why would a week-old car sell for thousands less?" judge false intro "What's your guess?"
        right: Hold that thought.
        * A week of wear and tear
        * The joy of 'new' is gone
        * Buyers wonder why it's for sale

A week barely scratches a car. Something bigger is going on.
    fade tag_new to 0.35

    erase tag_new tag_week

In 1970 the economist George Akerlof (@draw akerlof@) noted that the usual answer at the lunch table was the (@draw quote quote_by@) pure joy of owning a new car.

He offered  (@draw quote_x@)a different one.
    erase akerlof

Ask wh (@erase quote quote_by quote_x@)y anyone sells a car they bought a week ago. May (@highlight car effect pulse@)be they changed their mind. Or maybe they found (@draw lemon@) out it's a lemon.

B: Lemon is American slang for a dud. A good one, eco (@draw peach or@)nomists call a peach.

Only the  (@draw owner_cue@)owner knows which one it is. To a buyer, peache (@draw buyer@)s and lemons look the same on the lot.

So the buyer can only pay (@draw buyer_cue@) for an average car, with the chance of a lemon priced in.

B: Which is a bad deal for anyone selling a peach. Why let a good car go at a lemon-tinted price?
    highlight peach effect circle

    erase owner_cue

So peaches stay home, and  (@draw stay_cue@)the cars for sale get worse. That's where the trouble starts.
    move peach by [-140,0] duration 1.2

    pause 0.4

## The unravelling
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 10.6
    'y':
      - 0
      - 16
    box:
      x: 50
      'y': 165
      w: 610
      h: 480
vars:
    b:
      value: 1.5
      min: 1
      max: 2.5
      step: 0.1
      decimals: 1
    rnd:
      value: 1
      min: 0
      max: 8
      step: 1
      decimals: 0
    lag:
      value: 1
      min: 0
      max: 1
    P:
      expr: min(10, 10*pow(b/2, rnd))
    cut:
      expr: min(10, 10*pow(b/2, rnd - lag))
    half:
      expr: cut/2
    Pr:
      expr: 100*round(10*P)
    Ar:
      expr: 50*round(10*cut)
sources:
    - id: akerlof1970
      title: 'The Market for "Lemons": Quality Uncertainty and the Market Mechanism'
      authors: George A. Akerlof
      year: 1970
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 84(3): 488–500. In his example sellers value a car at its quality, buyers at 3/2 of it, quality uniform; with hidden quality the average quality on sale is half the price, so no trade takes place at any price. ''the bad driving out the not-so-bad driving out the medium driving out the not-so-good driving out the good in such a sequence of events that no market exists at all.'''

    axes ax x_label "cars, worst → best" y_label "value ($ thousand)" cross corner ticks.y [0,5,10,15] hidden true
    curve base expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 10 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    curve owner thick #b5482e expr x x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    label l_owner "worth to its owner" below-right #b5482e attach_to owner hidden true
    dot cutpt at.on owner at.x 10 bind {"at.x":"cut"} style.opacity 0 hidden true
    region forsale x_from 0 x_to.ref cutpt between ["owner","base"] style.fill #87a878 style.opacity 0.35 hidden true
    icon c1 at.data [0.5,2.2] size 58 set twemoji icon_look picture of automobile bind.size "max(0, 58*min(1, 1 + 4*(cut - 0.5)))" hidden true
    icon c2 at.data [1.5,3.2] size 58 set twemoji icon_look picture of automobile bind.size "max(0, 58*min(1, 1 + 4*(cut - 1.5)))" hidden true
    icon c3 at.data [2.5,4.2] size 58 set twemoji icon_look picture of automobile bind.size "max(0, 58*min(1, 1 + 4*(cut - 2.5)))" hidden true
    icon c4 at.data [3.5,5.2] size 58 set twemoji icon_look picture of automobile bind.size "max(0, 58*min(1, 1 + 4*(cut - 3.5)))" hidden true
    icon c5 at.data [4.5,6.2] size 58 set twemoji icon_look picture of automobile bind.size "max(0, 58*min(1, 1 + 4*(cut - 4.5)))" hidden true
    icon c6 at.data [5.5,7.2] size 58 set twemoji icon_look picture of automobile bind.size "max(0, 58*min(1, 1 + 4*(cut - 5.5)))" hidden true
    icon c7 at.data [6.5,8.2] size 58 set twemoji icon_look picture of automobile bind.size "max(0, 58*min(1, 1 + 4*(cut - 6.5)))" hidden true
    icon c8 at.data [7.5,9.2] size 58 set twemoji icon_look picture of automobile bind.size "max(0, 58*min(1, 1 + 4*(cut - 7.5)))" hidden true
    icon c9 at.data [8.5,10.2] size 58 set twemoji icon_look picture of automobile bind.size "max(0, 58*min(1, 1 + 4*(cut - 8.5)))" hidden true
    icon c10 at.data [9.5,11.2] size 58 set twemoji icon_look picture of automobile bind.size "max(0, 58*min(1, 1 + 4*(cut - 9.5)))" hidden true
    group cars members ["c1","c2","c3","c4","c5","c6","c7","c8","c9","c10"] hidden true
    dot avgpt #3d3833 at.on owner at.x 5 bind {"at.x":"half"} hidden true
    label l_avg "average" left attach_to avgpt hidden true
    curve offer dashed thick #2f6b8f expr P x_from 0 x_to 10.4 hidden true
    label l_offer "buyers offer" above-left #2f6b8f attach_to offer hidden true
    curve buyerv dashed #8a5fa8 expr b*x x_from 0 x_to 10 bind.x_to "min(10, 15.8/b)" style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    label l_buyerv "worth to a buyer" above-left #8a5fa8 attach_to buyerv hidden true
    region lost x_from 0 x_to 10 between ["buyerv","owner"] style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.4 hidden true
    text l_lost "gains never made" #a07d1c at.data [5.2,11] font_size 26 hidden true
    math rule tex "\\text{offer} = {b} \\times \\text{average}" colors {"\\text{offer}":"#2f6b8f"} x 835 y 600 hidden true
    math round_t tex \text{round}\;{rnd} x 835 y 530 hidden true
    text avg_t "average for sale: ${Ar:0,}" x 835 y 460 font_size 28 hidden true
    text offer_t "offer: ${Pr:0,}" #2f6b8f x 835 y 390 font_size 46 hidden true
    scale guess x 705 y 235 width 265 label "next offer ($ thousand)" min 0 max 10 value 5.6 hidden true
    text q1 "“…until no market" cites ["akerlof1970"] x 835 y 300 font_size 28 style.italic true hidden true
    text q2 "exists at all.”" x 835 y 260 font_size 28 style.italic true hidden true
    text q3 "— Akerlof, 1970" #8f887c x 835 y 220 font_size 22 hidden true
    scratch mathcard work [{"tex":"\\text{offer} = 1.5 \\times \\text{average}"},{"tex":"\\text{average} = \\tfrac{P}{2} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{offer} = 0.75\\,P"},{"tex":"b < 2:\\ \\text{shrinks} \\qquad b \\ge 2:\\ \\text{holds}"}] x 300 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "The unravelling"

If peaches stay home, how far do (@draw cars@)es the damage go? Line up ten used cars, from a near-wreck to almost new.
    draw ax

B: Each owner knows what theirs is  (@draw owner l_owner@)worth: from almost nothing up to  (@draw forsale@)ten thousand dollars. Illustrative numbers.

Buyers can't tell the cars apart, but a car is worth more to a buyer who needs one. Aker (@draw rule@)lof's own example: half as much again.
    highlight cars duration 2

B: With every car on sale,  (@draw avgpt l_avg avg_t@)the average is worth five thousand. So buy (@draw offer l_offer round_t offer_t@)ers offer one and a half times that: seven and a half.

    fade avg_t to 0

At seven and a half thousand, the owners of the best cars say no thanks. They keep them.
    animate lag 0 duration 2.5

B: Now the cars left for sale are worse on average, and the buyers know it.
    highlight avgpt

    draw guess

    ask "Click the line where you think the next offer lies." on guess store g relative true voice b
        intro: So what will buyers offer next?
        right: About 5.6 thousand: you said {g}.
        wrong: You said {g} thousand: here, about 5.6.

About five thousand six hundred. The cars left average three  (@fade avg_t to 1@)seven fifty, and half again is fifty-six hundred.
    animate rnd 2 lag 1 duration 2

    erase guess

And now the next-best owners leave. Round after round, the offer falls and another car drops out.
    animate lag 0 duration 1.2

    animate rnd 8 duration 5 easing linear

B: Akerlof's words: the bad driving out the not-so-bad, driving out (@draw q1 q2 q3@) the medium, until no market exists at all.

    erase q1 q2 q3

Yet in every round some buyer would gladly pay more than some owner wants. Trades (@draw lost l_lost@) that help both sides never happen.
    draw buyerv l_buyerv

Your turn: step through the rounds you (@highlight round_t effect box@)rself, then raise how much more buyers  (@highlight rule effect box@)would pay. Find where the market stops unravelling.
    animate rnd 1 duration 2

    explore

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own
        intro: Want to see why two is the line?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade ax owner l_owner forsale cars avgpt l_avg offer l_offer buyerv l_buyerv lost l_lost to 0.12

Buyers offer one and a half times the average car on sale.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1

If cars worth up to P are on sale, the average is worth half of P. So the next offer is three quarters of P.
    draw mathcard_line_2

Each round shrinks the offer by a quarter, all the way down. Only if buyers pay double or more does it hold.
    draw mathcard_line_3

    erase mathcard

@after

    erase mathq

    fade ax owner l_owner forsale cars avgpt l_avg offer l_offer buyerv l_buyerv lost l_lost to 1

## Beyond cars
sources:
    - id: akerlof1970
      title: 'The Market for "Lemons": Quality Uncertainty and the Market Mechanism'
      authors: George A. Akerlof
      year: 1970
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 84(3): 488–500. Section III, Examples and applications: A. insurance (''people over 65 have great difficulty in buying medical insurance''); B. the employment of minorities (race used as a statistic for job capabilities); C. the costs of dishonesty (''dishonest dealings tend to drive honest dealings out of the market''); D. credit markets in underdeveloped countries (Indian city banks at 6–10 per cent, local moneylenders at 15, 25 and even 50 per cent).'
    - id: nobel2001
      title: The Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel 2001
      authors: Nobel Foundation
      year: 2001
      finding: Awarded jointly to George A. Akerlof, A. Michael Spence and Joseph E. Stiglitz 'for their analyses of markets with asymmetric information'.

    box n1 "quality hidden" x 150 y 590 width 240 height 80 radius 14 font_size 30 hidden true
    box n2 "one price for all" x 520 y 590 width 270 height 80 radius 14 font_size 30 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    box n3 "the best walk away" x 790 y 250 width 280 height 80 radius 14 font_size 30 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    box n4 "average falls" x 250 y 250 width 240 height 80 radius 14 font_size 30 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    arrow e1 n1 -> n2 hidden true
    arrow e2 n2 -> n3 hidden true
    arrow e3 n3 -> n4 hidden true
    arrow e4 n4 -> n2 hidden true
    cards sorter bins ["The seller knows more","The buyer knows more"] check each hidden true
        * "Selling a used car" icon automobile bin "The seller knows more"
        * "Letting a flat to a tenant" icon house bin "The buyer knows more"
        * "Hiring a new worker" icon briefcase bin "The seller knows more"
        * "Buying life insurance" icon shield bin "The buyer knows more"
        * "An antique vase" icon amphora bin "The seller knows more"
    box case1 "insurance" cites ["akerlof1970"] x 520 y 410 width 320 height 80 radius 14 icon hospital icon_side left font_size 26 style.stroke #8f887c hidden true
    box case2 "hiring" cites ["akerlof1970"] x 520 y 410 width 320 height 80 radius 14 icon briefcase icon_side left font_size 26 style.stroke #8f887c hidden true
    box case3 "honest dealing" cites ["akerlof1970"] x 520 y 410 width 320 height 80 radius 14 icon handshake icon_side left font_size 26 style.stroke #8f887c hidden true
    box case4 "credit: 6–10% vs 50%" cites ["akerlof1970"] x 520 y 410 width 320 height 80 radius 14 icon "money bag" icon_side left font_size 26 style.stroke #8f887c hidden true
    text l_e4 "price falls" #8f887c x 285 y 440 font_size 26 hidden true
    text nobel "Nobel 2001" #2f6b8f cites ["nobel2001"] x 520 y 430 font_size 36 hidden true
    text nobel2 "Akerlof · Spence · Stiglitz" #2f6b8f x 520 y 385 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Beyond cars"

Strip away the cars and the unravelling is a (@draw n1@) loop. Quality is hidden, so every (@draw e1 n2@)one gets one price.

B: At that p (@draw e2 n3@)rice the best walk away, so (@draw e3 n4@) the average falls, th (@draw e4 l_e4@)e price falls, and (@flow e2 e3 e4 duration 2.5@) round again.

Akerlof's point was that the loop fits any market where one side knows more than the other. And it isn't always the seller.
    highlight n1 effect box

    fade n1 n2 n3 n4 e1 e2 e3 e4 l_e4 to 0

    draw sorter

    ask "Who knows more about the quality: the one selling, or the one buying? Sort each case." intro "Try it." on sorter store f stage own
        right: The buyer knows more in life insurance and the flat: the customer knows his own health and habits. The seller knows more about the car, the work and the vase.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. The customer knows his own health and habits: here, life insurance and the flat.

    erase sorter

    fade n1 n2 n3 n4 e1 e2 e3 e4 l_e4 to 1

B: Insurance was his first example: there the buyer knows h (@highlight n3 effect box@)is own health better than the insurer.
    draw case1

    erase case1

B: Hiring too. An employer who can't see a worker's skill may judge by a group label instead,  (@highlight n3 effect box@)and the skilled in that group lose out.
    draw case2

    erase case2

Then honesty. In his words, dishonest dealings tend to drive honest dealings out of the market.
    draw case3

    erase case3

B: And credit in India. Big city banks charged six t (@draw case4@)o ten per cent, village moneylenders up to fifty, because only a lender w (@highlight n1 effect box@)ho knows the borrower dares lend at all.

    erase case4

In 2001 Akerlof shared the No (@draw nobel nobel2@)bel prize with Michael Spence and Joseph Stiglitz, for markets with asymmetric information: o (@highlight n1 effect box@)ne side knowing more.

    pause 0.4

## Do lemons rule?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Not a lemon among your answers.
    good: Mostly peaches. A mechanic would sign that off.
    poor: A few lemons slipped through. Akerlof would say that's how it goes.
    none: Even Akerlof had three journals turn the idea down.
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 10.5
    'y':
      - 0
      - 12
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 430
      w: 400
      h: 225
sources:
    - id: akerlof1970
      title: 'The Market for "Lemons": Quality Uncertainty and the Market Mechanism'
      authors: George A. Akerlof
      year: 1970
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 84(3): 488–500. Section IV, Counteracting institutions: guarantees, brand names, chains, and licensing reduce quality uncertainty.'
    - id: bond1982
      title: 'A Direct Test of the "Lemons" Model: The Market for Used Pickup Trucks'
      authors: Eric W. Bond
      year: 1982
      finding: 'American Economic Review 72(4): 836–840. Using the 1977 Truck Inventory and Use Survey, trucks bought used needed no more maintenance than comparable trucks bought new (controlling for age and mileage): little evidence of a lemons market. A reanalysis by Pratt and Hoffer disputed this.'
    - id: peterson2014
      title: 'Adverse selection in the used-car market: evidence from purchase and repair patterns in the Consumer Expenditure Survey'
      authors: Jonathan R. Peterson and Henry S. Schneider
      year: 2014
      finding: 'RAND Journal of Economics 45(1): 140–154. Treating a car as a bundle of parts, they find adverse selection in the condition of the engine and transmission (and air-conditioning in colder months), but sorting, not adverse selection, in the vehicle body.'

    axes ax x_label cars y_label $ cross corner hidden true
    curve owner thick #b5482e expr x x_from 0 x_to 10 style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    curve offer dashed thick #2f6b8f expr "0.6" x_from 0 x_to 10.3 hidden true
    text theory "theory: no market" #2f6b8f x 260 y 395 font_size 32 hidden true
    icon lot1 size 100 set twemoji x 590 y 600 icon_look picture of automobile hidden true
    icon lot2 size 100 set twemoji x 720 y 600 icon_look picture of automobile hidden true
    icon lot3 size 100 set twemoji x 850 y 600 icon_look picture of automobile hidden true
    icon lot4 size 100 set twemoji x 590 y 495 icon_look picture of automobile hidden true
    icon lot5 size 100 set twemoji x 720 y 495 icon_look picture of automobile hidden true
    icon lot6 size 100 set twemoji x 850 y 495 icon_look picture of automobile hidden true
    group lot members ["lot1","lot2","lot3","lot4","lot5","lot6"] hidden true
    text reality "but: lots are full" #b5482e x 720 y 395 font_size 32 hidden true
    box fb1 "guarantee" cites ["akerlof1970"] x 210 y 240 width 250 height 80 radius 12 icon scroll icon_side left font_size 28 hidden true
    box fb2 "brand" cites ["akerlof1970"] x 500 y 240 width 250 height 80 radius 12 icon label icon_side left font_size 28 hidden true
    box fb3 "inspection" x 790 y 240 width 250 height 80 radius 12 icon wrench icon_side left font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch ev cites ["bond1982","peterson2014"] work ["Pickup trucks, 1982: few lemons","Engines, gearboxes, 2014: lemons","Bodywork you can see: none"] x 500 y 250 font_size 36 hidden true
    text st1 "can unravel a market: consensus" #3d3833 x 500 y 325 font_size 40 hidden true
    text st2 "how much it explains: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 265 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Do lemons rule?"

So in theory hidden quality (@draw theory@) can empty a market. But look outsi (@draw lot reality@)de: used-car lots are full.
    draw ax owner offer

B: Right. If the theory were true, shouldn't used cars have stopped selling decades ago?
    highlight lot effect box

Part of the answer is that markets fight back. Akerlof himself l (@draw fb1 fb2@)isted guarantees, brand names and licences.

B: Today add dealers with a reputation to  (@draw fb3@)lose, mechanics' inspections and the car's service history.

    erase fb1 fb2 fb3

And the test (@draw ev_box@)s? In 1982 Eric Bond compared used pickup trucks with ones kept from new, and (@draw ev_line_1@) found little sign of lemons.

B: A 2014 study looked part by part. In engines and ge (@draw ev_line_2@)arboxes, the parts a test drive can't reveal, it found lemons.

B: In the bodywork you can see, it did not.
    draw ev_line_3

    erase ev

So how sure are we? That hidden  (@draw st1@)quality can shrink or kill a market is consensus.

B: How much it explains of rea (@draw st2@)l used-car prices, that drop off the lot included, is contested.

So part of the drop when a car goes up for sale may come not from what changed, but from what selling it says: something only the owner knows.
    highlight lot effect box

    quiz "Mainland buyers can't tell Harbourtown's fresh fish from old, so all fish get one price. Whose catch leaves the market first?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: The freshest. One price for all pays the best fishers less than their fish is worth, so they sell elsewhere or not at all.
        wrong: Ask who loses most by selling at an average price: here, the freshest catch.
        + The freshest catch
        * The oldest catch
        * Both equally

    quiz "A website for second-hand phones starts paying for an independent check of every phone. What happens to the average phone on sale?" voice b
        right: It gets better. Once a good phone can prove it, its owner gets a fair price, and the peaches come back.
        wrong: Who was staying away before, and why? Here, it gets better.
        + It gets better
        * It gets worse
        * No change

    quiz "Which market is most at risk of unravelling?"
        right: The boat engine. Its faults are hidden, and a stranger you never meet again has no reputation to lose.
        wrong: Where can the buyer neither see the quality nor punish a cheat next time? Here, the boat engine.
        * Mia's bread, bought every morning
        + A used boat engine from a stranger
        * Petrol at the harbour pump

A market can fail with no smoke and no monopoly: just one side knowing more than the other.
    fade st1 st2 theory reality to 0.35

    pause 0.5

